From 9b4941b72f6a6149ea19ea1fa519928b63a635a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: facetimehd downstream <facetimehd-downstream@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 07/19] isp: validate everything the firmware hands back

The driver took firmware-supplied values on trust, and one of them was a
kernel pointer round trip: the shared-malloc handler wrote the address of an
isp_mem_obj into ISP memory and dereferenced whatever came back as a free
request. Firmware could name any address it liked.

Track ISP memory objects on a list under a new mem_lock and identify them by
offset instead. isp_mem_destroy_offset() frees the object at a given offset
and type, or reports -ENOENT and warns; isp_mem_destroy_all() reclaims the
whole list, which is what isp_uninit() now uses. The header written into
shared memory is a zeroed 64-byte block rather than a pointer.

The rest of the firmware-supplied data is checked where it arrives:

  - the channel descriptor table is range-checked before it is read, the
    channel count is bounded, and each channel's type, source, size and ring
    offset are validated against the mapped BAR before the ring is used;
    channel names are NUL-terminated.
  - the command response address must equal the request object we submitted,
    and a response may not be longer than the object holding it.
  - isp_init() fails if the firmware never established channel_io,
    channel_buf_h2t and channel_buf_t2h, instead of leaving NULL pointers
    for a later dereference.
  - CH_INFO_GET's sensor count is validated, and the camera-config sensor
    resolution is rejected unless it is plausible.
  - the firmware image is refused if it is larger than the preallocated
    region.

Ring and IRQ processing are bounded. fthd_handle_irq() drained the ring in a
while loop with no limit, so malformed data could keep a global workqueue
worker busy indefinitely; it now stops after chan->size entries and warns.
fthd_irq_work() reused its retry counter `i` as the channel-loop index, so
the 500-iteration escape hatch never worked as intended - the counters are
now separate. BUG_ON(chan->source > 3) on a firmware-supplied value becomes
WARN_ON_ONCE() and a skip.

Finally, the ring index is advanced after the entry is filled in rather than
before, so an entry cannot be published under an index the producer has
already moved past.
